Text:
This document specifies the form of a design document, and is itself a design document.
The implementation of a program is one layer.
The tests are a more abstract layer, but even to compile the tests need the structure of the implementation.
A design is more abstract still: a design has stories instead of tests, and grammar instead of the structure of the implementation.
Invariants, required directions of state transitions, pseudocode and, very occasionally, English text let a design abstract over many stories that would otherwise be needed.
Implementation notes bridge the gap to the implementation, or explain how an abstract step is possible.
A grammar is crucial for each shape that two programs share, and sometimes needed for an internal state.
The literature on user stories and on test driven development often overlooks grammar: in some domains code is mostly methods of existing datatypes, so the grammar comes for free.

Grammar:
Scope ::= Document1 .. Documentn
Document ::= Section1, "\n"+, Section2, .., "\n"+, Sectionn
Section ::= TextSection | GrammarSection | WellFormednessSection | StorySection | DefineSection | InvariantSection | ImplementationNotesSection
TextSection ::= "Text:\n", Line1 .. Linen
GrammarSection ::= "Grammar:\n", GrammarLine1 .. GrammarLinen
WellFormednessSection ::= "Well formedness:\n", WellFormednessLine1 .. WellFormednessLinen
StorySection ::= "Story ", StoryName, ":\n", StoryLines
DefineSection ::= "Define ", MethodHeader, ":\n", StoryLines
InvariantSection ::= "Invariant:\n", Line1 .. Linen
ImplementationNotesSection ::= "Implementation notes:\n", Line1 .. Linen
Line ::= AnyText, "\n"
GrammarLine ::= Nonterminal, " "*, "::=", Line
WellFormednessLine ::= "in", " "+, Nonterminal, ":", Line
StoryLines ::= StoryLine1 .. StoryLinen
StoryLine ::= Label, " ", Line
StoryName ::= [A-Za-z0-9]+
MethodHeader ::= Receiver, ".", MethodName, "(", AnyText, ")"
Receiver ::= [A-Za-z][A-Za-z0-9]*
MethodName ::= [a-z][A-Za-z0-9]*
Nonterminal ::= [A-Z][A-Za-z0-9]*
Label ::= [A-Z]
AnyText ::= [^\n]*

Well formedness:
in Scope: no two GrammarLine with equal Nonterminal.
in Scope: no two StorySection with equal StoryName.
in Document: only Sectionn can be an ImplementationNotesSection.
in Document: if Sectioni is a WellFormednessSection, Sectioni-1 is a GrammarSection holding a GrammarLine with the Nonterminal of each WellFormednessLine of Sectioni.
in StoryLines: the Label of StoryLinei is the i-th letter from A to Z.

Text:
The GrammarLines and the WellFormednessLines of all the Documents of a Scope form one grammar.
The 'right side' of a GrammarLine is the text after "::=": Pizza ::= something cooked in the oven is a GrammarLine.
A precise right side is a sequence of components separated by ", ", or alternatives separated by " | ".
A precise component is a Nonterminal, a string literal holding the text to match, a regular expression, X* or X+ for zero or more X or one or more X, or X1 .. Xn for a sequence of zero or more X.
The double quotes of a string literal can be left out when the text is clear without them: Infos.list(Project1 .. Projectn) stands for "Infos.list(", Project1 .. Projectn, ")".
A text matching the right side of N, but breaking a WellFormednessLine of N, is not an N.
For example, Infos.list(aa,aa) is not an Extensions, with this GrammarLine and this WellFormednessLine:
Extensions ::= Infos.list(Extension1 .. Extensionn)
in Extensions: all Extension are disjoint.
In the text after "in N:", a Nonterminal M stands for the parts of an N that are an M: Project and Projects stand for different parts.

Text:
A 'string literal' is the text between two double quotes of one line, with the escapes of the string literals of Java.
A 'comment' starts with a "//" outside every string literal, and ends at the "\n" of its line.

Define parser.check(f):
A f is a folder, and each file of f is in UTF-8, with "\n" at the end of each line.
B parser removes each line holding only spaces and a comment, with the "\n" of that line.
C parser removes each comment, with the spaces before that comment.
D parser removes each "\\" followed by "\n", with that "\n".
E parser checks that the files of f form a Scope.

Story StringLiteral:
A The folder f holds a file with the GrammarLine Url ::= "https://", Host
B parser.check(f) finds no comment in that GrammarLine.

Text:
A StoryLine is a condition, an action or an observed fact.
The StoryLines of a section happen, or hold, in the order of their Labels.
A condition holds from its StoryLine until a later StoryLine states a contradicting condition, or states that the condition does not hold any more.
Each DefineSection is one way a call of its MethodHeader can go.

Text:
A good design document starts with a few Text lines, followed by its GrammarSections and WellFormednessSections.
A good design document has no headings and no tables.
In a good design, each call in a StoryLine, such as parser.check(f), has a DefineSection.
